---
/**
 * Fender Jazz Bass dating guide. FIFTH OF THE MODEL-AXIS SPOKES.
 *
 * Sibling of the Stratocaster, Telecaster, Jazzmaster and Jaguar dating guides.
 * Built on joe/jazz-bass-dating-spoke, stacked on joe/jaguar-dating-spoke,
 * because every model page edits the hub's "Go Deeper" chips and the sitemap
 * list and would otherwise conflict.
 *
 * WHAT MAKES THE JAZZ BASS PAGE DIFFERENT. Do not clone the other four:
 *   - THE CONTROLS DATE THIS ONE, NOT THE HEADSTOCK. The dual-concentric
 *     "stack knob" layout ran from the 1960 launch to late 1961, so the single
 *     most decisive feature on the instrument is a CONTROL LAYOUT. No other
 *     model page has that. It is the spine of this page, the way the pickguard
 *     is the spine of the Jazzmaster page.
 *   - The logo does not date it either (transition logo 1960 THROUGH 1967,
 *     black from 1968), which is the same trap as the Jaguar. But the JAGUAR
 *     PAGE OWNS THAT FRAMING as its hook, so here it is a shorter section that
 *     cross-references the Jaguar rather than re-running the same argument.
 *   - THE 1970s BRIDGE PICKUP MOVE is unique to this model and nothing else on
 *     the site covers it: the bridge pickup moved about 0.4 inch toward the
 *     bridge in the early 1970s, taking pickup centres from ~3.6in to ~4.0in.
 *     This is the "60s spacing vs 70s spacing" people still buy pickups by.
 *   - MIXED PICKUP BOTTOMS ON A 1964 ARE CORRECT (one black, one grey) because
 *     the factory worked through stock. Say so; it is a common false alarm.
 *   - REVERSE-WIND KLUSONS to early 1966 tighten the "wrong" way. Bass-specific.
 *
 * JOE'S REVIEW RULES, carried over from the four sibling pages:
 *   - Features give a WINDOW, often wider than the serial. Only the neck heel
 *     date and the body date give a month.
 *   - The serial belongs in the second pass, read as one vote among several.
 *   - Never call a feature "model-specific" without checking it.
 *   - Never say "bare wood" about a body date. (It IS correct and is KEPT in
 *     the finish section, where it is the actual authentication point.)
 *   - Keep a photo caption on the one thing the photo is for.
 *
 * FACT SOURCING. Every dated claim traces to the Jazz Bass fact set added to
 * joes-vintage-databases as VG-0118 to VG-0130 (PR #4). Before that PR the
 * registry had ONE Jazz Bass entry and it was 1980s serial-prefix trivia.
 *   VG-0118 launch summer 1960        VG-0125 pickup bottoms black to grey mid-1964
 *   VG-0119 stack knob to late 1961   VG-0126 transition logo 1960-1967, black from 1968
 *   VG-0120 slab to veneer Jul-Aug 62 VG-0127 tuners: reverse / non-reverse / F stamp 1968
 *   VG-0121 clay to pearloid late 64  VG-0128 three-bolt + bullet truss rod late 1974
 *   VG-0122 binding late 1965         VG-0129 bridge pickup move, ~0.4 inch, early 1970s
 *   VG-0123 blocks mid-1966           VG-0130 maple + black blocks common from 1972
 *   VG-0124 pickguards + finish pairing
 *
 * FOUR OF THOSE ARE CORRECTIONS to Joe's first answers, accepted by him
 * 2026-08-10 and carrying verifyWithExpert:true in the registry (VG-0127 F
 * tuners 1968 not 1976; VG-0128 the bullet truss rod DID arrive; VG-0129 the
 * move is 0.4 inch not a tenth, and the YEAR is deliberately "the early 1970s"
 * because Joe says late 1972 and published sources say 1970/71; VG-0130 maple
 * blocks common from 1972, not late 1966). Do not tighten VG-0129's year to a
 * single figure without Joe ruling at the bench.
 *
 * PHOTO GAP, flagged to Joe: there is NO original-condition stack-knob control
 * plate photo and nothing between 1966 and 1972. The stack knob section is
 * illustrated by a REFINISHED 1961, used deliberately as a teaching photo.
 * If a clean stack knob turns up, it belongs in #controls.
 *
 * NO YEAR-TO-SERIAL-NUMBER TABLE, same as the four sibling pages.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LicensedImage from "../components/primitives/LicensedImage.astro";
import { resolveImage, assetSrc, ogCardSize } from "../lib/images";
import { contact, hrefs, SITE_URL } from "../config/site";

const meta = (file: string) => resolveImage("images/fender-sn/" + file)!;
const bmeta = (slug: string, file: string) => resolveImage(`images/blog/${slug}/${file}`)!;

const JB = "vintage-fender-jazz-bass-guide";

const PAGE_PATH = "/fender-jazz-bass-dating-guide/";
const HUB_PATH = "/fender-guitars-serial-number-guide/";
const HUB_HREF = HUB_PATH;
const OG = `images/blog/${JB}/1966-fender-jazz-bass-lake-placid-blue-1-scaled.jpg`;

// ---- Data ----
const yearRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  [
    "1960",
    "Launch, in the summer, as the deluxe two-pickup companion to the Precision and priced above it. Dual-concentric <a class='il' href='#controls'>stack knob controls</a>, a stacked volume and tone for each pickup. <a class='il' href='#neck'>Slab rosewood board</a> with clay dots, four ply tortoiseshell <a class='il' href='#guard'>guard</a>, black bottom pickups, reverse-wind Kluson tuners.",
    "Two stacked knobs instead of three separate ones. Nothing later looks like it.",
  ],
  [
    "1961",
    "The <a class='il' href='#controls'>stack knob layout</a> runs most of the year and is phased out late in it, replaced by the three-knob volume, volume and master tone arrangement on a single-ply plate. Everything else holds.",
    "Still two stacked knobs for most of the year. Late examples go to three.",
  ],
  [
    "1962",
    "Concentric basses do still leave the factory early in the year, built on leftover pre-drilled plates and bodies, and they are factory correct. By mid-year everything is three-knob. In July and August the slab rosewood board gives way to the thinner <a class='il' href='#neck'>veneer</a>.",
    "Rosewood depth at the nut. A <a class='il' href='#controls'>stack knob</a> this year is real, and uncommon.",
  ],
  [
    "1963",
    "No headline change. A 1963 carries the same specification as a late 1962, so this is a year to date from the <a class='il' href='#heel'>neck stamp</a> and the <a class='il' href='/fender-pot-codes/'>pot codes</a> rather than from across the room.",
    "Nothing visual. Go to the <a class='il' href='#heel'>heel stamp</a>.",
  ],
  [
    "1964",
    "Two changes, months apart. Around the middle of the year the pickup <a class='il' href='#bottoms'>bobbin flatwork</a> moves from black to grey, and the factory works through existing stock as it goes. Late in the year the clay <a class='il' href='#neck'>dots</a> give way to pearloid.",
    "Pull a pickup. And expect a mixed pair on this year, which is correct.",
  ],
  [
    "1965",
    "CBS completes the purchase in January but the bass changes in stages. Early to mid year the <a class='il' href='#guard'>pickguard</a> moves from nitrate to vinyl. Late in the year, around December, the fingerboard gains white <a class='il' href='#bounddot'>binding</a>.",
    "Binding on the board edge. Nothing before late 1965 has any.",
  ],
  [
    "1966",
    "Early production is the bound board still wearing pearloid dots. Around the middle of the year the pearloid <a class='il' href='#bounddot'>block inlays</a> arrive, and bound-and-blocked becomes the standard CBS neck.",
    "Bound with dots is early 1966. Bound with blocks is mid-1966 or later.",
  ],
  [
    "1967",
    "The specification holds through the year. The <a class='il' href='#seventies'>maple cap board</a> appears as a rare option, sometimes with black blocks and black binding, though it stays uncommon until the next decade.",
    "Nothing new. The <a class='il' href='#heel'>heel stamp</a> and the <a class='il' href='/fender-pot-codes/'>pots</a> do the work.",
  ],
  [
    "1968",
    "The thick black CBS <a class='il' href='#headstock'>logo</a> replaces the gold transition decal, and the Fender F stamped <a class='il' href='#tuners'>tuners</a> replace the Klusons.",
    "A black logo and a large stylized F on the tuner back plates. Both mean 1968 or later.",
  ],
  [
    "1969 to 1971",
    "The specification holds. Bound rosewood, pearloid blocks, black logo, F tuners, polyester finishes.",
    "Nothing to separate these years without the <a class='il' href='#heel'>internal dates</a>.",
  ],
  [
    "1972 to 1974",
    "One piece <a class='il' href='#seventies'>maple necks</a> arrive in 1972 and the maple board with black block inlays and black binding becomes the look of the decade. Somewhere in the early 1970s the <a class='il' href='#spacing'>bridge pickup moves</a> about 0.4 inch closer to the bridge.",
    "Black blocks in black binding on maple. Correct here, and rare before it.",
  ],
  [
    "1975",
    "The <a class='il' href='#seventies'>three-bolt Micro-Tilt</a> neck joint and the bullet truss rod arrive, from late 1974 for the 1975 model year. A few basses still leave with four-bolt necks from leftover stock.",
    "Count the neck bolts, then look for the bullet at the headstock.",
  ],
];

// The checks that place a Jazz Bass in an era without tools. These give a WINDOW.
// NOTE the first row: on this model a CONTROL LAYOUT is the fastest check, which
// is not true of any other Fender model page on this site.
const fastRows: [string, string, string, string][] = [
  ["The controls", "Stack knob to late 1961, three knob after", "Two stacked concentric knobs or three separate ones. Stack knob basses carry the biggest premiums on the model, and the answer reads from any photograph of the body.", "#controls"],
  ["The fingerboard binding", "None to late 1965, white binding after", "A white line down each edge of the board. The cleanest CBS marker here, same as on the offsets.", "#bounddot"],
  ["The inlays", "Clay dots to late 1964, pearloid dots to mid-1966, pearloid blocks after", "Clay is matte and chalky. Pearloid is bright and reflective. Blocks mean a bound board too.", "#neck"],
  ["The pickguard", "Four ply nitrate to early or mid 1965, three ply after", "Hold the edge to a strong light and count the layers. Nitrate also shrinks, cracks and smells of vinegar.", "#guard"],
  ["The pickup bottoms", "Black fibreboard to about mid-1964, grey after", "Worth the screwdriver on an early bass. Expect a mixed pair on a 1964, which is correct rather than a swap.", "#bottoms"],
];

// Jazz Bass neck-heel model code, from the Joe-corrected table on the neck-dates spoke.
const codeRows: [string, string][] = [
  ["7", "Jazz Bass, 1962 to 1965, in the rubber-stamped heel codes"],
  ["15", "Jazz Bass from 1966, after Fender renumbered the model codes at the end of 1965"],
];

// Tuner eras.
const tunerRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Reverse-wind Kluson", "1960 to early 1966", "Open gear with a clover leaf pad. Turns clockwise to tighten, which is backwards to a modern player and is correct here."],
  ["Non-reverse Kluson", "Early 1966 to 1968", "The same clover leaf pad, turning the standard direction."],
  ["Fender F stamped", "1968 to 1975", "A large stylized F on the back plate. Fender made these in-house at first; Schaller only took over manufacture in the mid 1970s."],
];

const tocItems: [string, string][] = [
  ["#start", "Start Here"],
  ["#fast", "The Five Fastest Checks"],
  ["#years", "Jazz Bass Year By Year"],
  ["#controls", "Stack Knob Or Three Knob"],
  ["#headstock", "What The Headstock Won't Say"],
  ["#neck", "Board, Inlays And Binding"],
  ["#guard", "The Pickguard"],
  ["#pickups", "Pickups And The Spacing Change"],
  ["#tuners", "Tuners"],
  ["#heel", "The Neck Heel Date"],
  ["#body", "Body Dates"],
  ["#serial", "What The Serial Number Adds"],
  ["#seventies", "The 1970s Changes"],
  ["#limits", "What Features Can't Prove"],
  ["#cross", "Confirm It From The Inside"],
  ["#faq", "Common Questions"],
  ["#appraisal", "Free Expert Appraisal"],
  ["#about-joe", "About Joe"],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Jazz Bass?",
    a: "Work it in three passes and expect each to give you a window rather than an answer. Start with the controls and the fingerboard, because between them they cut the run into slices from across the room: stack knob or three knob, bound or unbound, dots or blocks. Then read the pickguard layers, the tuners, and the serial number together, treating the serial as one vote among several. Then open it up for the neck heel stamp, the body date, and the pot codes, which are the only places Fender wrote a real date and the only things that will give you a month. One warning specific to this model: the headstock is close to useless for dating, so do not start there.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Stack Knob Jazz Bass And Why Is It Worth So Much?",
    a: "It is one of the first ones. From the 1960 launch until late 1961 the Jazz Bass used dual-concentric controls, a stacked volume and tone pair for each pickup, before Fender simplified to the three-knob volume, volume and master tone layout. That is roughly eighteen months of production out of a run that never really stopped, which is what drives the price. Concentric basses built in early 1962 on leftover pre-drilled plates are also factory correct and rarer still. Because the control plate is four screws away from being somebody else's, confirm a stack knob against the neck heel date, the body date and the pot codes before you pay stack knob money.",
  },
  {
    q: "I Have A Concentric Jazz Bass Dated 1962. Is It A Fake?",
    a: "Probably not. Fender phased the stack knob layout out in late 1961, but Leo Fender did not throw away usable stock, so pre-drilled control plates and already-routed bodies kept going out the door into early 1962. An early 1962 stack knob is a genuine factory build. What it needs is corroboration: the internal dates should land in the same window, and the plate should sit on routing and screw holes that match it with nothing filled underneath. By the middle of 1962 everything was three-knob, so a concentric bass with a late 1962 or 1963 neck date is a different conversation.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does The Headstock Logo Tell Me If My Jazz Bass Is Pre-CBS?",
    a: "No, and it is the most common way this model gets misdated. The gold transition logo ran on the Jazz Bass from 1960 all the way through 1967, so it covers the entire pre-CBS era and the first three years of CBS ownership. A 1962 and a 1967 wear the same decal. What the logo does tell you is the 1968 line, because the thick black CBS logo arrives then and a black logo means 1968 or later. Everything else has to come from the controls, the board, the hardware and the dates inside. The Jaguar has exactly the same problem for the same reason.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Jazz Bass Has A Bound Board With Dot Inlays. Is That Wrong?",
    a: "No, that is a factory build. White binding arrived around December 1965 and the pearloid block inlays did not follow until about the middle of 1966, so there is a real window where a bound board still wears dots. The same binding-before-blocks gap shows up on the Jaguar and the Jazzmaster, so it is a Fender-wide pattern rather than a quirk of this model. Anybody telling you a bound board must have blocks is working from a rule that skipped a step.",
  },
  {
    q: "One Pickup Has A Black Bottom And The Other Is Grey. Has One Been Replaced?",
    a: "If the bass is a 1964, almost certainly not. Fender changed the pickup bobbin flatwork from black fibreboard to grey around the middle of 1964 and worked through existing stock as it went, so a mixed pair on a 1964 is correct factory output. The same overlap turns up on Stratocasters and Precision Basses of the period. On a 1962 or a 1968 the same mixed pair is worth a much harder look, because the changeover was long finished by then.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why Does My 1970s Jazz Bass Sound Brighter Than A 1960s One?",
    a: "Mostly the bridge pickup, and it is a measurable difference rather than a matter of taste. On 1960s basses the two pickups sit about 3.6 inches apart measured centre to centre. In the early 1970s Fender moved the bridge pickup about 0.4 inch closer to the bridge, taking that spacing out to roughly 4.0 inches, and returned to the earlier placement in the early 1980s. Moving a pickup closer to the bridge makes it brighter and more cutting, which is a large part of the 1970s Jazz Bass voice. It is also why replacement pickup sets are still sold as 60s spacing or 70s spacing, and why buying the wrong one leaves you with a pickup that does not line up under the strings.",
  },
  {
    q: "Are Three-Bolt Necks A Problem?",
    a: "Not inherently. The three-bolt Micro-Tilt neck joint arrived in late 1974, for the 1975 model year, together with the bullet truss rod adjuster at the headstock, and Fender went back to four bolts in 1983. The joint earned a poor reputation from neglected examples with worn neck pockets, where three bolts hold a sloppy fit worse than four would. A well maintained one is stable, and the 1970s basses have a real following now. Check the pocket fit and the neck angle rather than dismissing it on principle. A four-bolt 1975 or 1976 is not automatically wrong either, because leftover stock was used up during the changeover.",
  },
];

// ---- Schema ----
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How To Date A Fender Jazz Bass, Year By Year",
  description:
    "Date a vintage Fender Jazz Bass from the features you can see: the stack knob controls, the binding, the inlays, the pickguard and the pickup bottoms, year by year from 1960 to 1975.",
  author: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/about-me/#person` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/` },
  image: `${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`,
  datePublished: "2026-08-10",
  dateModified: "2026-08-10",
  inLanguage: "en-US",
  mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE_URL}${PAGE_PATH}`,
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": `${SITE_URL}${HUB_PATH}` },
};
const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: SITE_URL + "/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Fender Serial Number Guide", item: `${SITE_URL}${HUB_PATH}` },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Jazz Bass Dating Guide", item: `${SITE_URL}${PAGE_PATH}` },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="How To Date A Fender Jazz Bass: Year By Year Guide"
  description="Date a vintage Fender Jazz Bass from what you can see: stack knob or three knob, the binding, the inlays, the pickguard and the pickup bottoms, 1960 to 1975."
  canonical={PAGE_PATH}
  ogImage={`${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`}
  {...ogCardSize(OG)}
  ogImageAlt="A 1966 Fender Jazz Bass in Lake Placid Blue custom color"
  ogType="article"
  noIndex={false}
  structuredData={[articleSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <!-- Archetype B header backdrop: solid bands the absolute site Header sits on -->
  <div class="fspot-header-backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__dark"></div>
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__cream"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="fspot">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <a class="back" href={HUB_HREF}>Back To The Full Fender Serial Guide</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Fender Dating By Model</span>
        <h1>How To Date A Fender Jazz Bass</h1>
        <p class="lead">Count the knobs first. On every other vintage Fender the fastest check is something on the neck or the headstock, but the Jazz Bass splits at the front of its run on a control layout: two stacked concentric knobs to late 1961, three separate ones after. That single question separates the most valuable basses of the run from everything that followed, and you can answer it from any photograph of the body. Here is what changed and when, from the summer 1960 launch through 1975.</p>
        <div class="trust">
          <span>Joe Dampt</span><span>12 years buying vintage Fenders full-time</span><span>10,000+ appraised</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hubcta">
          <p class="hubcta__t"><b>Just after the serial number?</b> The full charts and the lookup tool that decodes a number for you are on the main guide.</p>
          <a class="hubcta__b" href={HUB_HREF}>Look Up A Serial Number</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="sfig herofig">
          <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1964-fender-jazz-bass-original-owner-scaled.jpg")} alt="The original owner of a 1964 Fender Jazz Bass holding it, showing the sunburst body, tortoiseshell pickguard, unbound rosewood board with dot inlays, and the three knob chrome control plate" width={1400} loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>A 1964 with the man who bought it new. Three knobs, tortoiseshell guard, unbound dot board: the middle of the run in one photograph.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- START HERE -->
    <section id="start">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Start Here</span>
        <h2>Read The Bass In Three Passes</h2>
        <p>Nothing in the first two passes will hand you a single year, and it helps to know that going in. Binding only says late 1965 or later. Blocks only say mid-1966 or later. Each feature is a window, and several are wider than the serial number is. What makes the Jazz Bass friendlier than most is that its changes are unusually well spaced, so stacking three or four of them usually lands you inside a couple of years.</p>
        <div class="passes">
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass One</span>
            <h3>Across The Room</h3>
            <p>The controls and the fingerboard. Stack knob or three knob, bound or unbound, dots or blocks. Those questions alone place most examples.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#controls">The controls</a>
              <a href="#neck">The board and inlays</a>
              <a href="#bounddot">The binding</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass Two</span>
            <h3>In Your Hands</h3>
            <p>The pickguard layers, the tuners, and the serial number. Take the serial as one vote among several rather than the answer, and read the plate style along with the digits.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#guard">The pickguard</a>
              <a href="#tuners">The tuners</a>
              <a href="#serial">The serial number</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pass">
            <span class="pass__n">Pass Three</span>
            <h3>Inside The Bass</h3>
            <p>The neck heel stamp, the body date, and the pot codes, plus a pickup pulled to read the bottom colour on an early bass. These are the only places Fender wrote an actual date.</p>
            <div class="pass__go">
              <a href="#heel">The neck heel date</a>
              <a href="#body">Body dates</a>
              <a href="#bottoms">Pickup bottoms</a>
              <a href="/fender-pot-codes/">Pot codes</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p><b>The three passes should agree.</b> When they do, you have a build date you can stand behind. When they don't, the disagreement is the interesting part, and on this model it most often means a control plate, a neck, or a set of pickups that came from somewhere else.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SAFETY -->
    <section class="pt0" aria-label="Safety warning">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="warnbox" role="note">
          <span class="warnbox__head"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"></path><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path></svg>Read This First</span>
          <p>The whole first pass reads with the bass assembled. If you do go in, sixty-year-old nitrate pickguards are brittle and crack at the screw ears, and the control plate on an early bass is the last thing you want to mark. If the outside checks leave you stuck between two years, contact me before you take anything apart.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- FIVE FASTEST -->
    <section id="fast">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Short Version</span>
        <h2>The Five Fastest Checks</h2>
        <p>If you read nothing else on this page, read these five. Read them together rather than one at a time: no single row narrows anything to a year, but five rows overlapping do.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>What to look at</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>How to read it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{fastRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="What to look at"><a class="rowjump" href={r[3]}>{r[0]}</a></td><td data-label="What it tells you" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="How to read it">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The top row is the one that pays.</b> On a Stratocaster or a Telecaster the fastest check is the neck or the headstock. Here it is the control plate, and the difference between the two answers is measured in five figures rather than percentages. Everything else on this page narrows the year. That first row decides which conversation you are having.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- YEAR BY YEAR -->
    <section id="years">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Whole Timeline</span>
        <h2>Jazz Bass Year By Year, 1960 To 1975</h2>
        <p>What changed each year, and the quickest way to see it. Two things to keep in mind while you read down the table. Fender never made a clean cutover, so a change dated to mid-year means both versions turn up on instruments from that year and a transition example is genuinely correct either way. And parts went into a bin before they went into a bass, so a feature dates the part, and the instrument is that year or later.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What changed</th><th>Fastest tell</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{yearRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Year" class="yr">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="What changed" set:html={r[1]}></td><td data-label="Fastest tell" set:html={r[2]}></td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="note-sm">For the long version of any row, including the custom colour checks and the value drivers, the <a class="spoke" href="/post/vintage-fender-jazz-bass-guide/">vintage Jazz Bass guide</a> runs the whole model in detail.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- CONTROLS: THE SPINE -->
    <section id="controls">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Spine Of The Bass</span>
        <h2>Stack Knob Or Three Knob</h2>
        <p>The Jazz Bass did not launch with the layout everybody pictures. The first ones carried <b>dual-concentric controls</b>, usually called <b>stack knobs</b>: two stacked knobs, each one a volume and a tone for a single pickup. Four controls in two positions, and a look nothing else in the Fender line shared.</p>
        <p>Fender phased it out in <b>late 1961</b> and replaced it with the three-knob volume, volume and master tone arrangement that the model has used ever since. So the run splits at the very front, and it splits on something you can see in any photograph of the body.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>A 1962 concentric bass is not automatically a fake.</b> Leo Fender did not throw away usable stock, so pre-drilled control plates and already-routed bodies kept going out the door into <b>early 1962</b>. Those are factory correct and rarer than the 1960 and 1961 basses. By the middle of 1962 everything leaving Fullerton was three-knob, so a concentric bass with a late 1962 or 1963 neck date is telling you a different story.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1961-fender-jazz-bass-stack-knob-refinished-black.jpg")} alt="A refinished 1961 Fender Jazz Bass stack knob in black with a tortoiseshell pickguard, standing in a grey lined case, with heavy wear showing bare wood directly beneath the black paint and no undercoat layer" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>A 1961 stack knob. The concentric controls are correct and original. The black finish is not: the wear breaks straight from paint to bare wood with no undercoat and no sealer line underneath it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p><b>The control plate is four screws.</b> That is the whole problem with a feature this valuable. Before you pay stack knob money, confirm the layout against the neck heel date, the body date and the pot codes, and pull the plate to check that the routing and the screw holes match it with nothing filled underneath. A correct plate on a body that was drilled for three knobs is not subtle once the plate is off.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- HEADSTOCK -->
    <section id="headstock">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">A Known Trap</span>
        <h2>What The Headstock Won't Tell You</h2>
        <p>Only two logos ever went on a vintage Jazz Bass. The <b>gold transition logo</b> ran from the 1960 launch <b>through 1967</b>, and the thick <b>black CBS logo</b> from <b>1968</b>. That is the whole story, and it means the decal covers the entire pre-CBS era and the first three years of CBS ownership in one style. A 1962 and a 1967 wear the same logo.</p>
        <p>So the headstock answers one question and only one: <b>a black logo means 1968 or later</b>. It cannot tell you whether a bass is pre-CBS, which is the question people are usually asking when they look at it. This catches sellers as often as buyers, and a listing that leans on the decal to justify a pre-CBS price is worth a second look at everything else.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1973-fender-jazz-bass-sunburst-front-of-headstock-scaled.jpg")} alt="The front of the headstock on a 1973 Fender Jazz Bass showing the thick black CBS logo reading Fender Jazz Bass, with a maple neck and four in line tuners" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The black CBS logo on a 1973. This decal is the one piece of headstock information that dates anything: black means 1968 or later.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jaguar-dating-guide/#headstock">Jaguar has the same problem for the same reason</a>, and that page goes into how the patent block picks up the slack when the decal cannot. The logo styles across the whole Fender line are on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/#logo">parts and features guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK -->
    <section id="neck">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass One</span>
        <h2>The Board, The Inlays And The Binding</h2>
        <p><b>Slab or veneer.</b> Look along the side of the neck at fret level, where the rosewood meets the maple. A slab board shows a flat seam with rosewood of even thickness. A veneer board shows a curved seam, thinner rosewood following the radius beneath it. The Jazz Bass changed over in <b>July to August 1962</b>. Worth being precise rather than reaching for a rule of thumb: the Stratocaster moved around the same time, but the Jazzmaster went to veneer in the spring of 1962, several months earlier. Fender's changes did not all land on one date.</p>
        <p><b>Clay to pearloid dots</b> happened in <b>late 1964</b>, the same window as the rest of the line. Clay reads matte and chalky and ages toward tan or cream; pearloid is brighter, whiter and visibly reflective.</p>

        <h3 id="bounddot">Binding First, Then Blocks</h3>
        <p>Here is the sequence people run together, and the two changes are about six months apart. <b>White fingerboard binding arrived in late 1965</b>, around December. <b>Pearloid block inlays did not follow until the middle of 1966.</b> That leaves a genuine window where a Jazz Bass carries a <b>bound board with dot inlays</b>, and those necks are factory correct rather than parts guitars.</p>
        <p>The same binding-before-blocks gap turns up on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jaguar-dating-guide/#bounddot">Jaguar</a> and the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jazzmaster-dating-guide/#inlays">Jazzmaster</a>, so it is a Fender-wide pattern rather than a quirk of this model. Anybody telling you a bound board must have blocks is working from a rule that skipped a step.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1973-fender-jazz-bass-block-inlays.jpg")} alt="Close up of the maple fingerboard on a 1973 Fender Jazz Bass showing white binding, bold black block inlays, and amber toned maple under the lacquer" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>Blocks and binding on a 1973 maple board. Pearloid blocks on bound rosewood arrived mid-1966; this black-on-maple version is the 1970s look.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- PICKGUARD -->
    <section id="guard">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>The Pickguard</h2>
        <p>Hold the guard edge to a strong light and count the layers. Jazz Bass guards moved from <b>nitrate to vinyl in early to mid 1965</b>, with leftover nitrate stock overlapping the change. Tortoiseshell guards are <b>four ply</b>; white, parchment and black guards are <b>three ply</b>.</p>
        <p>The pairing rule answers a question that comes up constantly. <b>Sunburst basses took the four ply tortoiseshell guard. Custom colours took a three ply white or parchment guard.</b> So a white guard on a custom colour Jazz Bass is correct and original rather than somebody's later swap. Tortoiseshell does turn up on selected custom finishes such as Olympic White by special order, which is a real exception rather than an excuse, and it needs the rest of the bass to support it.</p>
        <p>The nitrate guards also age in a way reproductions do not manage. They shrink, cup, crack at the screw ears, and outgas an acidic vapour you can smell the moment you open a case that has been shut a while. That vinegar tang is a genuine check. The vinyl guards that replaced them do not shrink, do not smell, and have no mint green underside.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- PICKUPS -->
    <section id="pickups">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Under The Covers</span>
        <h2>Pickups, And The Change Nobody Mentions</h2>

        <h3 id="bottoms">Black Bottoms Versus Grey</h3>
        <p>Pull a pickup and look at the flatwork. Fender used <b>black fibreboard from 1960 to about the middle of 1964</b>, then switched to <b>grey</b> for the rest of the run. Original black bottoms in an early bass carry a premium, so on anything sold as 1960 to early 1964 this is the check worth making before you agree a price.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>A mixed pair on a 1964 is correct.</b> One black bottom and one grey bottom in the same bass is not evidence that somebody replaced a pickup, it is the factory working through existing stock during the changeover. The same overlap shows up on Stratocasters and Precision Basses of the period. On a 1962 or a 1968 the same mixed pair deserves a much harder look, because the change was long finished by then.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 id="spacing">The 1970s Bridge Pickup Move</h3>
        <p>This is the Jazz Bass change that explains a tonal difference people hear long before they know why, and it is the one thing on this page that has no equivalent on any other model.</p>
        <p>On 1960s basses the two pickups sit about <b>3.6 inches apart</b>, measured centre to centre. In the <b>early 1970s</b> Fender moved the <b>bridge pickup about 0.4 inch closer to the bridge</b>, taking that spacing out to roughly <b>4.0 inches</b>. Fender returned to the earlier placement in the early 1980s.</p>
        <p>Moving a pickup toward the bridge makes it brighter and more cutting, and that shift is a large part of the 1970s Jazz Bass voice. It is also why replacement pickup sets are still sold as <b>60s spacing</b> or <b>70s spacing</b>: buy the wrong one and the pole pieces do not line up under the strings. Measure it before you assume, because it is a small number on paper and obvious in the hands.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1973-fender-jazz-bass-bridge.jpg")} alt="The chrome bridge of a 1973 Fender Jazz Bass with four threaded saddles and the strings anchored through the bridge plate" width={1280} />
            <figcaption>The bridge on a 1973. Measuring from the bridge pickup pole pieces to the saddles is the fastest way to separate 60s spacing from 70s spacing on an unmarked bass.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- TUNERS -->
    <section id="tuners">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>Tuners</h2>
        <p>Three eras, and the transitions are clean enough to be useful on their own.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Tuner</th><th>Years</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{tunerRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Tuner"><b>{r[0]}</b></td><td data-label="Years" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="What to look for">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The <b>reverse-wind</b> tuners on the early basses are the ones that catch players who have only handled modern instruments, because they tighten the opposite way round. That is correct on a pre-1966 Jazz Bass and they are worth keeping. Tuner replacement is one of the most common modifications on any vintage Fender, usually because fifty-year-old gears develop slop, so check the peg holes for enlargement or for filled secondary holes left by a set with a different gear ratio.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK HEEL -->
    <section id="heel">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>The Neck Heel Date</h2>
        <p>Take the four neck plate bolts out and the heel carries a date. It is the most defensible build date on the instrument, because it records when the neck was actually shaped and finished. The stamp runs a model code, then the month, then the year, then a letter for the nut width.</p>
        <p>Ignore the model code for the dating itself, but read it anyway, because on this model it moved:</p>
        <div class="tablewrap compact"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{codeRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Code" class="code">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="What it means">{r[1]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Fender renumbered the model codes at the end of 1965, so a Jazz Bass neck from 1965 opens with a 7 and one from 1966 opens with a 15. That makes the code a genuine cross-check here, the same way it is on a Stratocaster and unlike the Telecaster, whose code never moved.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">How the stamp format changed across the whole line, and the full model code table, are on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-neck-dates/">neck date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- BODY -->
    <section id="body">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>Body Dates</h2>
        <p>The other place Fender wrote a real date, written directly on the body inside a cavity. It corroborates rather than decides: bodies sat in the warehouse before being matched to necks, so a body date and a neck date a few months apart is the normal result on an untouched bass. Read it as a floor.</p>
        <p>What you do not want to see is paint sitting <b>over</b> the date stamp rather than under it, which means the body has been refinished.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">Where the date hides on every Fender model, and what the pencil, ink and tape markings look like, is on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-body-dates/">body date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SERIAL -->
    <section id="serial">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two, One Vote</span>
        <h2>What The Serial Number Adds</h2>
        <p>A useful vote, and no more than that. Fender stamped neck plates in batches, stored them, and pulled them off the stack in no particular order, so a plate could sit on a shelf for months and a higher number could go on a bass built before a lower one. Published ranges overlap year to year and disagree between sources. Read the plate alongside the features rather than instead of them, and treat a mismatch as the interesting part.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1973-fender-jazz-bass-sunburst-neck-plate-scaled.jpg")} alt="The neck plate of a 1973 Fender Jazz Bass showing the stamped serial number on chrome" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>The neck plate on a 1973. One vote among several, and four screws away from being somebody else's.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">There is no Jazz Bass serial chart on this page because Fender serials are not model-specific. A 1963 neck plate reads exactly the same whether it ended up under a Jazz Bass, a Stratocaster or a Jaguar, so the charts live once on the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a> rather than being copied onto every model page.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SEVENTIES -->
    <section id="seventies">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Last Stretch</span>
        <h2>The 1970s Changes</h2>
        <p><b>One piece maple necks arrived in 1972</b>, and with them the look most people picture on a 1970s Jazz Bass: a maple board with <b>black block inlays and black binding</b>. That combination existed earlier as a rare maple cap option, from about 1967 to 1968, but it stayed uncommon until 1972. From then it is factory correct and it is the defining appearance of the decade, not a replacement neck.</p>
        <p><b>The three-bolt Micro-Tilt neck joint arrived in late 1974</b>, for the 1975 model year, together with the <b>bullet truss rod</b> adjuster at the headstock. Fender went back to a four-bolt joint in 1983. A handful of 1975 and 1976 basses did leave with four-bolt necks from leftover stock, so a four-bolt bass from those two years is not automatically wrong, just worth checking against the neck date.</p>
        <p>The three-bolt joint has a reputation it only partly deserves. It earned that reputation from neglected examples with worn neck pockets, where three bolts hold a sloppy fit worse than four would. A well maintained one is stable, and the 1970s basses have their own following now, helped along by the brighter bridge pickup placement covered above.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1973-fender-jazz-bass-sunburst-full-shot-of-front-scaled.jpg")} alt="Full front view of a 1973 Fender Jazz Bass in sunburst with a maple neck, black block inlays and a black pickguard" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>A 1973. Maple board, black blocks, black binding, and the four-bolt neck that ran until late 1974.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- LIMITS -->
    <section id="limits">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Keep It Honest</span>
        <h2>What The Features Can't Prove</h2>
        <p>Every feature on this page dates a part. None of them dates the bass. On a model where a stack knob plate carries the money it does, that gap is exactly what a parts assembly exploits.</p>
        <p><b>The control plate is the loudest feature and the easiest to move.</b> That is not a theoretical worry on this model, it is the specific problem a stack knob attracts, because the premium is large and the plate is four screws. Treat the layout as the thing that starts the conversation rather than the thing that settles it, and expect three internal dates to agree with it.</p>
        <p><b>A correct feature set is not proof of originality.</b> Originality is a whole-instrument judgment that weighs the neck date, the body date, the pots, the pickup bottoms, the guard material and the finish against each other and asks whether they tell one story. A well done parts assembly passes any single check on this page.</p>
        <p><b>Transition dates are approximate, and honest instruments break them.</b> Fender phased changes in as bins emptied, so a bass from a changeover year can legitimately carry either spec or one of each. On a Jazz Bass that lands hardest on early 1962, where a concentric bass is correct and valuable, and on 1964, where a mixed pair of pickups is normal rather than suspect.</p>
        <p>The finish is where the most value moves. On a custom colour, look at how the wear breaks: an original finish shows the colour over a sealer layer and often an undercoat, so wear that goes straight from paint to bare wood with no layers between is telling you the body was sprayed outside the factory.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(JB, "1966-fender-jazz-bass-lake-placid-blue-back-wear-white-undercoat.jpg")} alt="Back of a 1966 Fender Jazz Bass in Lake Placid Blue showing heavy belt wear where the blue has worn through to reveal a white undercoat beneath" width={1400} />
            <figcaption>Wear on a 1966 Lake Placid Blue. The white undercoat showing under the blue is exactly right for a metallic, which needed a bright base to read correctly.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- CROSS -->
    <section id="cross">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Put It All Together</span>
        <h2>Confirm It From The Inside</h2>
        <p>The <a class="spoke" href="#heel">neck heel date</a> and the <a class="spoke" href="#body">body date</a> above are the two that give you a month. Two more parts back them up. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pot-codes/">pot codes</a> date a potentiometer to the week, which is the tightest number anywhere on the bass, though it is a floor rather than a build date because pots sat in bins. Two pots more than about six months apart from each other deserve a second look. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pickup-dates/">pickup dates</a> corroborate when they are present, and on a Jazz Bass the bottom colour does real work alongside them. For the parts view across every Fender model rather than this one, work through <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/">dating a Fender by its parts</a>.</p>
        <p>For the full history of this model, including the custom colour checks, the red flags and the value drivers, the <a class="spoke" href="/post/vintage-fender-jazz-bass-guide/">vintage Jazz Bass guide</a> is the long version of everything here. Its stablemate runs the opposite way on two checks: the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-precision-bass-dating-guide/">Precision Bass</a> never took binding or block inlays in standard production, and it kept a four-bolt neck right through the 1970s, so the three-bolt plate that is correct on a late 1974 Jazz Bass is a red flag on a Precision. The other sibling model pages built the same way as this one are the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-stratocaster-dating-guide/">Stratocaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-telecaster-dating-guide/">Telecaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jazzmaster-dating-guide/">Jazzmaster</a>, <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jaguar-dating-guide/">Jaguar</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/fender-mustang-dating-guide/">Mustang</a> dating guides.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">This guide is one of the model pages behind the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a>, where the complete year-by-year serial charts live.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Expert Questions</span>
        <h2>Common Questions About Dating A Jazz Bass</h2>
        <div class="faqs">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="faq">
              <summary>{f.q}</summary>
              <div class="faqbody"><p>{f.a}</p></div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- APPRAISAL -->
    <section>
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="appraise" id="appraisal">
          <span class="eyebrow">Free Expert Appraisal</span>
          <h2>Think You Have A Stack Knob?</h2>
          <p>They only ran from 1960 to late 1961, plus a few into early 1962, and the control plate is four screws away from being somebody else's. That makes them worth checking properly. Send Joe three clear photos, the front, the back and the headstock, for a free expert appraisal within 24 hours. No obligation, and no screwdriver required.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="call" href={hrefs.tel}>Call Or Text Joe: {contact.phone}</a>
            <a class="send" href="/free-appraisal/">Or Send Photos Online</a>
          </div>
          <p class="note-sm">Thinking about selling? Read how to <a class="spoke" href="/sell-my-fender-guitar/">sell your Fender</a>, or see the <a class="spoke" href="/vintage-fender-stratocaster-value-guide/">Stratocaster</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/vintage-fender-telecaster-value-guide/">Telecaster</a> value guides for a sense of how the vintage market runs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BIO -->
    <section class="pt0">
      <div class="measure"><div class="bio" id="about-joe">
        <LicensedImage src={meta("about-me-joe-1.jpg")} alt="Joe Dampt, owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars, holding a vintage guitar" width={150} />
        <div>
          <div class="who"><a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a></div>
          <div class="cred">Owner, Joe's Vintage Guitars · Mesa, AZ · 12 years full-time · 10,000+ appraised · 2,100+ positive reviews</div>
          <p class="biotext">Joe Dampt is the owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars in Mesa, Arizona, where he's spent more than twelve years buying, appraising, and authenticating vintage instruments full-time. He's personally valued over 10,000 guitars for sellers in all 50 states, served as a traveling appraiser for the Treasure Seekers Roadshow, and sourced instruments for Grammy Award-winning artists. Every check on this page comes from that bench work.</p>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>
  </div>

